 ## Subtopics
 
-### [Surface-based Instancing with Noise Modulation](1582-surface-based-instancing-with-noise-modulation.md)
-Generates high-density point clouds from surface primitives to achieve smooth volumetric appearance at high resolutions. It differs from the base method by explicitly instantiating a massive number of points (e.g., ~400 million) per primitive and modulating their density or position using procedural noise functions before rasterization or voxelization [1582-surface-based-instancing-with-noise-modulation.md]. Choose this when a smooth, organic volumetric look is required instead of hard-edged cubes.
+### Sphere Impostors for DEM Pebbles
+Renders Discrete Element Method (DEM) pebble beds using instanced sphere impostors [S2, unofficial]. Each pebble is drawn as a quad with an exact orthographic ray-sphere hit test in the fragment shader, providing sphere shading and depth [S2, unofficial]. It differs from standard voxel cubes by using a 2D primitive with a 3D intersection test in the fragment shader, allowing for smooth spherical appearance without the geometry overhead of actual spheres [S2, unofficial]. It is chosen for visualizing large numbers of discrete particles (e.g., 27,000 pebbles) at interactive rates with low CPU overhead (1.2-1.3 ms per frame) [S2, unofficial].
 
-### [3D Gaussian Splatting via Instancing](1586-3d-gaussian-splatting-via-instancing.md)
-Renders 3D Gaussian splats by submitting instanced geometry (quads) to the standard hardware vertex/fragment pipeline, avoiding the memory overhead of tile-based rasterization. It solves the problem of efficiently projecting 3D ellipses onto the screen by dynamically sizing quads based on opacity cutoffs and evaluating the Gaussian decay function in the fragment shader. It differs from [Volumetric Spot Noise Rendering](2360-volumetric-spot-noise-rendering.md) by using explicit deterministic paths and geometry instancing for depth/opacity accumulation rather than stochastic noise patterns. Choose this for neural radiance field (NeRF) or Gaussian splat visualization.
-
-### [GPU Instancing with Indirect Draw Calls](2205-gpu-instancing-with-indirect-draw-calls.md)
-A technique to render a large number of identical meshes (voxels) efficiently by using indirect draw calls. Instead of issuing one draw call per instance, a single draw call is issued that reads instance counts and parameters from a GPU-accessible buffer, allowing the GPU to generate the draw commands itself. It is distinguished from its siblings by decoupling the CPU-side draw call issuance from the GPU-side execution logic, enabling dynamic instance counts and reducing CPU-GPU synchronization overhead compared to standard hardware instancing. Choose this when instance counts are dynamic or extremely large, and CPU overhead is a bottleneck.
-
-### [Indirect Draw Argument Setting](2418-indirect-draw-argument-setting.md)
-Uses a persistently mapped Vertex Buffer Object (VBO) as a memory pool for interleaved vertex data, divided into fixed-size buckets. Each occupied bucket is associated with an indirect draw call structure, allowing multiple sub-meshes to be rendered simultaneously via `glMultiDrawElementsIndirect`. This method reduces driver overhead from frequent state changes and draw calls while simplifying memory management for procedural voxel meshing. It sets itself apart from naive per-chunk VAO/VBO systems by merging sub-meshes and from merged VAO/VBO systems by avoiding full buffer resizes and re-uploads when updating sub-regions. Choose this for complex scenes with many distinct material groups or chunks.
-
-### [Procedural Particle Rendering](2926-procedural-particle-rendering.md)
-Generates particle attributes on-the-fly by seeding a random number generator (RNG) with a particle ID, eliminating the need to store particle data (position, velocity, color, size) in memory buffers. This method solves the memory bandwidth and storage overhead associated with traditional particle systems, allowing for the generation of thousands of particles directly within a shader. It is distinct from [Instancing with Procedural/Shader Generation](2794-instancing-with-procedural-shader-generation.md) by specifically focusing on the stochastic initialization of particle states rather than geometric variation, and differs from [Procedural Point Rendering with Billboard Transformation](6422-procedural-point-rendering-with-billboard-transformation.md) by deriving dynamic motion via physics equations rather than static billboard transformations. Choose this for fluid, smoke, or fire effects where particle data is too large to store.
-
-### [WebGL 2 Hardware Instancing](3152-webgl-2-hardware-instancing.md)
-Sends a single base primitive (typically a 2x2x2 cube) to the GPU and executes `gl.drawElementsInstanced` to render hundreds of thousands of copies simultaneously. It solves the CPU bottleneck associated with issuing individual draw calls for large numbers of voxels by packing translation, scaling, and color data into flat typed arrays. Unlike surface-based instancing which modulates geometry with noise, this method relies on explicit instance buffers and procedural sampling (e.g., from heightmaps) to dictate vertex positions and colors. Choose this for WebGL-based voxel applications requiring broad compatibility.
-
-### [Geometry Shader Advanced Instancing for Cubes](4576-geometry-shader-advanced-instancing-for-cubes.md)
-A technique using geometry shaders to generate cube meshes from instanced points, optimizing for visibility by emitting at most 12 vertices (3 quads) per cube. It differs from creating full 24-vertex cubes by leveraging the fact that backface culling always removes one of two opposite faces, allowing the shader to emit only the visible subset. Choose this when vertex shader instancing is insufficient and you need to reduce vertex count per visible voxel.
-
-### [Procedural Point Rendering with Billboard Transformation](6422-procedural-point-rendering-with-billboard-transformation.md)
-A rendering technique using Unity procedural instancing to render surface points as billboards. It transforms a grid-based point cloud into world space, orients each point to face the camera, and scales them based on a step size. Choose this for point-cloud-based voxel visualization where orientation to the camera is critical.
-
-### [Buffer-Based Instancing (SSBO/Instance Buffers)](6501-buffer-based-instancing-ssbo-instance-buffers.md)
-Replaces standard hardware per-instance vertex buffers with Shader Storage Buffer Objects (SSBOs) or Structured Buffers, allowing the vertex shader to procedurally look up instance-specific data (such as position, rotation, and material properties) using the `gl_InstanceID`. It solves the problem of memory bandwidth and driver overhead associated with large numbers of instances by enabling dense, custom-packed data layouts and procedural geometry generation. It differs from standard hardware instancing by relying on an index-based lookup into a shader-accessible buffer rather than a second vertex buffer attached to the draw context, which allows for greater flexibility in data structure but typically incurs higher latency due to the lack of hardware precaching [11005]. Choose this when instance data is complex, sparse, or requires procedural generation on the GPU.
+### Splat quad vertex layout
+Sets up a 4-vertex quad with specific coordinates $(-2,-2), (2,-2), (2,2), (-2,2)$ and indices $0,1,2,0,2,3$ for shader-based scaling of projected 2D Gaussian eigenvectors [S3, unofficial]. The vertex buffer uses `VET_FLOAT2` for position, and the instance buffer uses `VET_FLOAT1` for texture coordinates [S3, unofficial]. This layout is used in 3D Gaussian Splatting via instancing to efficiently project ellipses onto the screen [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
--   **Time Complexity:** Setup time is $O(V)$ to compute offsets and pack data, where $V$ is the number of voxels [S1, unofficial]. Draw time is $O(1)$ draw call, with fragment shader cost proportional to the number of visible voxels [S1, unofficial].
--   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$ for the instance buffer (5 floats per voxel) and $O(P)$ for the palette texture, where $P$ is the number of unique colors/materials [S1, unofficial].
--   **Performance:** Significantly reduces CPU overhead compared to per-voxel draw calls. Performance scales linearly with the number of visible voxels.
--   **Strengths:** Low memory footprint for instance data, efficient use of GPU instancing, simple implementation.
--   **Limitations:** Limited to uniform voxel shapes (cubes). Modifying instanced voxels based on position (e.g., environmental effects) is not practical as it requires processing time and additional storage to remember changes, negating the speed and memory benefits [S2, unofficial]. Coordinate system mismatches (e.g., Z-up vs Y-up) must be handled manually [S1, unofficial]. Palette texture sampling artifacts can occur at boundaries if wrap modes are not set correctly [S1, unofficial].
--   **When to Use:** Ideal for static or semi-static voxel models where memory bandwidth and CPU draw call overhead are bottlenecks.
--   **When Not to Use:** When voxels require significant per-voxel variation (e.g., dynamic environmental effects) or non-cubic shapes.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Setup time is $O(V)$ to compute offsets and pack data, where $V$ is the number of voxels [S1, unofficial]. Draw time is $O(1)$ draw call with instancing, but fragment shader cost is proportional to the number of visible voxels [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$ for the instance buffer (5 floats per voxel) and $O(P)$ for the palette texture [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Implemented in the VoxelRenderer Android application for rendering `.vly` models using OpenGL ES 3.0 [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Low CPU overhead for draw calls; efficient memory usage for large voxel counts; flexible material assignment via palette textures [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires a palette texture; back-face culling is necessary for performance; coordinate system conversions must be handled manually [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When rendering large, static voxel models with simple shading requirements and limited GPU memory bandwidth [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** When dynamic, per-voxel geometry changes are frequent (requires buffer updates) or when complex, non-uniform lighting per voxel is needed beyond simple Phong [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Originating from the need to render large voxel worlds efficiently, this method leverages hardware instancing capabilities available in modern graphics APIs (OpenGL ES 3.0, WebGL 2) [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. It was notably implemented in the VoxelRenderer Android application for rendering .vly voxel models [S1, unofficial]. The concept of voxel instancing, where a "class" of voxels is stored once and instances reference it, was discussed in the context of procedural world generation to reduce memory footprint and increase rendering speed [S2, unofficial].
+This method is implemented in the VoxelRenderer Android application for rendering `.vly` voxel models using OpenGL ES 3.0 [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the principles of [Voxels rasterized as geometry](27-voxels-rasterized-as-geometry.md) by using instancing to reduce draw call overhead [S1, unofficial].
 
